WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.255
Lesson 2.0.

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:05.635
The Mastery Starter for Module 2: Trading On-Chain.

00:00:05.635 --> 00:00:07.439
By the end, you'll know the five

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:09.571
words this module runs on, and exactly

00:00:09.571 --> 00:00:11.539
how to tell when you've mastered it.

00:00:12.839 --> 00:00:14.129
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:17.448
First, the sixty-second version: what actually happens

00:00:17.448 --> 00:00:20.404
when you swap or provide liquidity on-chain.

00:00:20.404 --> 00:00:22.081
Second, the five words you'll

00:00:22.081 --> 00:00:23.779
need, each one drawn out.

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:26.194
Third, what to have ready before you start.

00:00:26.194 --> 00:00:28.740
Fourth, your first safe step: comparing a

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.949
real quote across two venues, without executing.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.464
And finally, the mastery ladder, so you know

00:00:34.464 --> 00:00:37.034
exactly what finishing this module looks like.

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:40.899
Here's the whole module in one sentence.

00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:42.854
A swap trades against a pool of

00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:44.932
tokens priced by a formula, not a

00:00:44.932 --> 00:00:46.759
person on the other side.

00:00:46.759 --> 00:00:49.513
Providing liquidity to that same pool earns

00:00:49.513 --> 00:00:51.578
you a share of trading fees, but

00:00:51.578 --> 00:00:54.319
it also changes what you actually hold.

00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:57.168
This module teaches you to do both deliberately,

00:00:57.168 --> 00:00:59.494
knowing the real cost before you commit.

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:02.767
Here's what happens the moment you hit

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:05.444
“swap.” You're not trading with another person.

00:01:05.444 --> 00:01:08.184
You're trading against a pool of two tokens,

00:01:08.184 --> 00:01:10.498
held by a smart contract, priced by a

00:01:10.498 --> 00:01:13.782
formula that shifts as the pool's balance shifts.

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:16.054
Your trade itself moves that price, a

00:01:16.054 --> 00:01:18.386
little or a lot depending on size.

00:01:18.386 --> 00:01:21.337
That's the entire mechanism underneath every

00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:22.976
DEX trade in this module.

00:01:24.276 --> 00:01:26.304
And here's why this module exists

00:01:26.304 --> 00:01:28.454
at all, in one honest sentence.

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:30.155
The quoted price on screen is

00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:32.099
never quite what you receive.

00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:35.255
Price impact, pool fees, slippage and gas all take

00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:37.855
a bite between the number you see and the

00:01:37.855 --> 00:01:40.754
number that actually lands in your wallet.

00:01:40.754 --> 00:01:43.499
Knowing exactly how much, before you trade,

00:01:43.499 --> 00:01:45.933
is the entire skill this module builds.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:49.008
Now, the five words you'll need.

00:01:50.308 --> 00:01:53.547
Word one: DEX, a decentralised exchange.

00:01:53.547 --> 00:01:55.492
Here's what actually happens when you

00:01:55.492 --> 00:01:57.122
use one, from your wallet.

00:01:57.122 --> 00:02:00.587
You connect your wallet directly, no account or sign-up.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:03.157
You choose the two tokens you want to trade.

00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:05.299
The DEX quotes you a price, pulled

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:07.312
from its pools in real time.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:10.800
And you sign one transaction, which settles instantly,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.288
on-chain, with no intermediary ever holding your funds.

00:02:15.588 --> 00:02:17.523
Word two: liquidity pool.

00:02:17.523 --> 00:02:20.699
A pot of two tokens that traders swap against,

00:02:20.699 --> 00:02:24.513
funded by people called liquidity providers, or LPs.

00:02:24.513 --> 00:02:27.199
Deposit both tokens in the pool's ratio, and

00:02:27.199 --> 00:02:29.228
you receive a share, and a cut of

00:02:29.228 --> 00:02:32.273
every trade's fee, proportional to your share.

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:33.934
But your share of the pool can

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.648
end up worth a different mix of

00:02:35.648 --> 00:02:37.448
tokens than what you put in.

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:38.933
That's word five, coming up.

00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:42.258
Word three: price impact.

00:02:42.258 --> 00:02:44.512
Not a fee, a mechanical consequence

00:02:44.512 --> 00:02:46.878
of trade size against pool depth.

00:02:46.878 --> 00:02:48.822
A small trade against a deep pool

00:02:48.822 --> 00:02:50.838
barely moves the price at all.

00:02:50.838 --> 00:02:52.944
The same trade against a shallow pool

00:02:52.944 --> 00:02:55.438
moves it noticeably, because you're a bigger

00:02:55.438 --> 00:02:57.678
share of what's actually available.

00:02:57.678 --> 00:03:00.641
Bigger trade, or shallower pool, means more

00:03:00.641 --> 00:03:03.401
price impact, every time, by the formula

00:03:03.401 --> 00:03:05.623
itself, not by anyone's decision.

00:03:06.923 --> 00:03:09.713
Word four: slippage, and it's easy to confuse

00:03:09.713 --> 00:03:13.106
with price impact, so here's the actual difference.

00:03:13.106 --> 00:03:15.384
Price impact is what your trade itself

00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:17.312
does to the pool's price, a real

00:03:17.312 --> 00:03:19.131
cost baked into the trade.

00:03:19.131 --> 00:03:21.927
Slippage tolerance is a setting you choose: the

00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:25.247
worst price you'll accept before your transaction simply

00:03:25.247 --> 00:03:28.043
reverts, protecting you from a price that moves

00:03:28.043 --> 00:03:30.431
between your quote and your confirmation.

00:03:31.731 --> 00:03:34.031
One real, worked number for a liquidity

00:03:34.031 --> 00:03:36.171
pool, from later in this module.

00:03:36.171 --> 00:03:38.841
Put ten thousand dollars into a pool as a

00:03:38.841 --> 00:03:41.892
zero point one percent share, and a typical day

00:03:41.892 --> 00:03:45.452
might earn around six dollars in fees, which annualises

00:03:45.452 --> 00:03:49.757
to roughly twenty-two percent, before impermanent loss and gas.

00:03:49.757 --> 00:03:52.645
That's the number Lesson two point three teaches you

00:03:52.645 --> 00:03:55.587
to calculate for real, on any pool you're considering.

00:03:56.887 --> 00:03:57.977
Quick check.

00:03:57.977 --> 00:04:00.009
What's the actual difference between

00:04:00.009 --> 00:04:01.987
price impact and slippage tolerance?

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:08.607
The answer: price impact is what your

00:04:08.607 --> 00:04:10.702
trade does to the pool's price.

00:04:10.702 --> 00:04:13.433
Slippage tolerance is a setting you choose: the

00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:16.675
worst price you'll accept before the transaction reverts.

00:04:17.975 --> 00:04:21.550
Word five: impermanent loss, from Lesson two point four.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.315
Here's the honest shape of it.

00:04:23.315 --> 00:04:25.370
Hold both tokens yourself, and your

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:27.440
value simply tracks the market.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.798
Provide the same two tokens as liquidity instead, and

00:04:30.798 --> 00:04:33.970
if their price ratio moves apart, your LP position

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.142
ends up worth less than simply holding would have,

00:04:37.142 --> 00:04:39.930
even after counting the fees you earned.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.267
It's called “impermanent” because it only

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:44.270
becomes permanent once you withdraw.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.615
Before you start.

00:04:47.915 --> 00:04:50.110
Two things, both already covered.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.416
First, Modules Zero and One done: a secured setup,

00:04:53.416 --> 00:04:56.917
and safe wallet habits already in place, since you'll

00:04:56.917 --> 00:04:59.615
be signing real swaps in this module.

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:02.630
Second, a little E.T.H. for gas, and some

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:06.160
U.S.D.C. to trade with, on a low-fee network.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.544
If either is missing, go

00:05:07.544 --> 00:05:09.395
back and finish those first.

00:05:09.395 --> 00:05:12.110
This module builds directly on those habits.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:14.685
Your first safe step.

00:05:15.985 --> 00:05:18.217
Your first step in this module doesn't

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.005
touch a real trade at all.

00:05:20.005 --> 00:05:22.375
Quote a twenty-dollar swap on one DEX

00:05:22.375 --> 00:05:24.496
directly, and the same swap on an

00:05:24.496 --> 00:05:27.665
aggregator, which searches many pools for you.

00:05:27.665 --> 00:05:30.989
Compare what you'd actually receive after gas, on each.

00:05:30.989 --> 00:05:32.774
Don't execute either one.

00:05:32.774 --> 00:05:35.006
You're building the habit of comparing net

00:05:35.006 --> 00:05:36.979
received, before the habit of trading.

00:05:38.279 --> 00:05:40.524
Here's exactly what you're comparing.

00:05:40.524 --> 00:05:42.314
A single DEX quotes you one

00:05:42.314 --> 00:05:44.404
price, direct from one pool.

00:05:44.404 --> 00:05:47.001
An aggregator searches many pools and venues,

00:05:47.001 --> 00:05:49.259
and may split your order across several

00:05:49.259 --> 00:05:51.479
routes to get a better net result.

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:53.877
Neither is always better; it depends on

00:05:53.877 --> 00:05:55.735
the size of your trade and how

00:05:55.735 --> 00:05:57.774
liquidity is spread that day.

00:05:57.774 --> 00:06:00.799
The habit is checking both, every time, not assuming.

00:06:02.099 --> 00:06:03.842
One more routing trade-off worth

00:06:03.842 --> 00:06:06.149
knowing while you're comparing quotes.

00:06:06.149 --> 00:06:10.164
A single-pool route touches one contract, simple and predictable,

00:06:10.164 --> 00:06:13.384
but it might not be the cheapest path available.

00:06:13.384 --> 00:06:15.626
A multi-hop route, say, token A to B

00:06:15.626 --> 00:06:17.626
to C, can land a better price by

00:06:17.626 --> 00:06:20.959
spreading across pools, but it touches more contracts,

00:06:20.959 --> 00:06:23.928
meaning more that would need to behave correctly

00:06:23.928 --> 00:06:25.504
for your trade to succeed.

00:06:26.804 --> 00:06:27.894
Quick check.

00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:30.700
Why compare a DEX quote against an aggregator

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:33.749
quote before trading, instead of just picking one?

00:06:38.169 --> 00:06:41.149
The answer: an aggregator searches many pools and

00:06:41.149 --> 00:06:43.415
can split your order for a better net

00:06:43.415 --> 00:06:46.276
result, but it isn't always better, so checking

00:06:46.276 --> 00:06:48.839
both tells you what you'd actually receive.

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:51.234
The mastery ladder.

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:55.559
Here's how you'll measure your progress through Module Two.

00:06:55.559 --> 00:06:58.094
Beginner: makes small swaps with deliberate

00:06:58.094 --> 00:07:00.699
slippage settings and protected routing.

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:04.900
Practitioner: calculates fee A.P.R. and impermanent loss, and

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:08.859
actively tracks an LP position against simply holding.

00:07:08.859 --> 00:07:13.636
And Master: uses limit, T.W.A.P. and intent-based orders, and

00:07:13.636 --> 00:07:17.643
runs LP positions with written entry and exit rules.

00:07:18.944 --> 00:07:21.544
Here's the road ahead, lesson by lesson.

00:07:21.544 --> 00:07:25.264
2.1, DEXs, aggregators and routing. 2.2, AMM mathematics.

00:07:25.264 --> 00:07:28.017
2.3, providing liquidity.

00:07:28.017 --> 00:07:30.502
2.4, impermanent loss and true LP P&L.

00:07:30.502 --> 00:07:34.962
2.5, MEV and protecting your trades.

00:07:34.962 --> 00:07:38.582
2.6, advanced execution: limit, TWAP and intent orders.

00:07:38.582 --> 00:07:44.301
2.7, advanced AMM design and LVR.

00:07:44.301 --> 00:07:51.491
And 2.8, the MEV supply chain.

00:07:52.791 --> 00:07:55.796
One more preview, from Lesson two point five.

00:07:55.796 --> 00:07:58.596
Some of what looks like slippage is actually

00:07:58.596 --> 00:08:01.832
M.E.V., other traders exploiting the fact that your

00:08:01.832 --> 00:08:05.243
pending transaction is visible before it confirms.

00:08:05.243 --> 00:08:07.658
A “sandwich” attack buys just ahead of

00:08:07.658 --> 00:08:10.196
your trade and sells just after, pushing

00:08:10.196 --> 00:08:12.168
your price worse both ways.

00:08:12.168 --> 00:08:14.391
Protecting your trades rounds out this

00:08:14.391 --> 00:08:16.158
module for exactly that reason.

00:08:17.458 --> 00:08:20.514
You've mastered this module when you can quote a

00:08:20.514 --> 00:08:23.820
swap's real cost, and an L.P. position's real result

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:27.738
against simply holding, before you ever commit either one.

00:08:27.738 --> 00:08:29.143
Not a big trade.

00:08:29.143 --> 00:08:31.893
A repeatable habit of knowing the number first.

00:08:33.193 --> 00:08:34.233
One more.

00:08:34.233 --> 00:08:36.630
You provide liquidity to a pool, and the

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.503
two tokens' prices drift apart over a month.

00:08:39.503 --> 00:08:41.183
Even after counting the fees you

00:08:41.183 --> 00:08:42.913
earned, what's this effect called?

00:08:47.333 --> 00:08:50.323
The answer: impermanent loss, how far your LP

00:08:50.323 --> 00:08:53.573
position lags simply holding the same two tokens,

00:08:53.573 --> 00:08:56.693
and it only becomes permanent once you withdraw.

00:08:57.993 --> 00:08:59.178
Last check.

00:08:59.178 --> 00:09:01.793
What does mastering Module Two actually look like?

00:09:06.213 --> 00:09:08.774
The answer: quoting a swap's real cost,

00:09:08.774 --> 00:09:11.400
and an LP position's real result against

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.153
holding, before you ever commit either one.

00:09:15.453 --> 00:09:17.408
Let's recap the five words.

00:09:17.408 --> 00:09:20.783
DEX: you trade against a pool, no intermediary.

00:09:20.783 --> 00:09:23.442
Liquidity pool: deposit both tokens, earn a

00:09:23.442 --> 00:09:25.857
fee share, but your mix can change.

00:09:25.857 --> 00:09:29.612
Price impact: your own trade moving the price, mechanically.

00:09:29.612 --> 00:09:32.817
Slippage: the worst price you'll accept, your own setting.

00:09:32.817 --> 00:09:35.317
And impermanent loss: how far an LP

00:09:35.317 --> 00:09:38.790
position lags simply holding, once prices diverge.

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:41.785
That's the Mastery Starter.

00:09:41.785 --> 00:09:44.344
You now have the whole module in your head:

00:09:44.344 --> 00:09:46.902
what a swap and an LP position actually do,

00:09:46.902 --> 00:09:49.577
the five words, your first safe step, and the

00:09:49.577 --> 00:09:52.145
ladder that tells you when you're done.

00:09:52.145 --> 00:09:56.210
Next up, Lesson 2.1: DEXs, aggregators and routing.
